WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.120
 Hello and welcome to the transport layer. We're working our way up from the bottom of our four

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:10.000
 layer architecture up to the top. So we're kind of at the halfway point. We've covered

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:14.240
 the link layer like ethernet and then we covered the internet work layer, which is kind of like

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:21.600
 the postcard layer. So let's, let's review the magic of IP, right? The magic IP is this postcard

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:28.160
 layer that bounces these packets with from addresses and to addresses through, you know,

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:34.720
 15 or so hops, getting them there, getting packets to their destination network from one network to

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:38.800
 another network as well as it can. And then when it gets to the destination network, it finds the

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:47.920
 final computer on that network. The magic of this is there is no interim long-term storage inside

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:52.720
 the network. All the long-term storage is outside. And now we're going to talk about that. So one of

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:58.000
 the things that makes IP so fast is that it is not demanded to be perfect. It is not demanded

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:04.000
 to deliver data in order. And it's not demanded to, there's no requirement that it doesn't lose data.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:08.880
 It's fast and barely ever loses data, but when it does, there's a layer to recover. And that's what

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:18.320
 we're going to talk about next. So the internet protocol is this multi hop packets can take

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:24.960
 multiple paths. They can make use of all kinds of crazy links, but now we're going to move up one,

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:32.640
 right? We're going to move up to the TCP layer. So the TCP layer is both simple and complex.

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:39.920
 The purpose of the TCP layer is to compensate for the possible errors in the IP layer, as well as

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:50.800
 make best use of available resources. So if the overall network from here to here is extremely

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:55.360
 fast, we want to send data really fast, right? If on the other hand, the overall network from

00:01:55.360 --> 00:02:00.560
 here to here is really slow, we want to send data slowly and be efficient. Because remember,

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:06.320
 part of the goal of the TCP IP networking is to share effectively. And so we need to be aware of

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:11.280
 whether our network is fast or slow. And those are the kinds of problems we solve the TCP layer. How

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:19.440
 fast is the underlying network? How reliable is it? And if something goes wrong, what do we do to

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:27.360
 deal with that? So the key idea in TCP IP is that when we send some data, we break it into packets,

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:35.840
 and then we send each one, and then we keep them until they get an acknowledgement from the other

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:41.760
 side. And then and only then do we throw them away. And at some point, if a packet gets lost,

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:47.760
 it can be sent again, and again, and again, until it finally is acknowledged in the destination

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:54.800
 system. And so that's basically what TCP does is it figures out which packets have or have not made

00:02:54.800 --> 00:03:01.520
 it across the internet layer. So here's an example scenario. So we got a message, it's broken up into

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:08.320
 five packets, we've got sort of the first 100 characters, the first characters, the second,

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:16.720
 and third packet. Now, what TCP does is it speculatively sends a few packets says, okay,

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:21.280
 let's let's get ahead, because if you sent one and waited, then you might not make best use the

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:26.560
 network. So you kind of guess and dump a few packets out there. And so you send let's say we

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:31.440
 send three packets, queue them up for sending and as fast as we can send them out the back of our

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:36.400
 network, we start spooling them out the back of their network. And somehow two of them make it

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:42.560
 across the internet to the destination, but somehow that poor second packet just like every

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:46.480
 time I'm doing a lecture, the poor second packet never gets it. You don't want to be the second

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:50.480
 packet in one of my lectures, because you're going to get it. But somehow the second packet

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:58.400
 has gotten vaporized. Now time passes. And the receiver gets this

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:06.080
 sensation that maybe, maybe, maybe something's missing. And so it sends an acknowledgement back.

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:12.640
 And it says, I'm going to send a note back that says, look, I'm ready for 200. I do have 100 and

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:17.840
 I'm ready for 200. Matter of fact, I may even throw away 300 because it's been so long.

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:26.720
 So I want you to start me over at 200. But sender all of a sudden knows that 100 has been sent.

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:30.800
 And so it knows that it can throw this one away now, right? It's been acknowledged.

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:35.280
 So there's an acknowledgement stream that goes back and forth. So now the sender

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:43.760
 says 200 and 300. And then they make it across. The receiver says, I've got 200.

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:49.920
 This is the 300. And then they send 400 speculatively. 300 makes it, 400 makes it.

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:56.720
 And the receiver says, I got 400, which means now it can cross off 300 and 400, right? Check those

00:04:56.720 --> 00:05:03.360
 off and throw them away and then send 500 cross. And then at some point check those off. It's got

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:08.240
 500. And now the sender can sort of like empty, check everything off. Everything's been sent.

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:12.480
 And it's been acknowledged and we know. And so that's kind of an oversimplified view of the kind

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:22.400
 of bookkeeping that's going on on both sides of a TCP connection. And so this right here is in a

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:29.840
 sense, the genius of the internet, the storage requirements in the middle, the routers, this is

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:37.280
 the IP basically, the network of networks. We didn't design these to require a lot of storage.

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:41.600
 We wanted them to be fast. We wanted them to be agile. We want to be dynamic. We wanted to be

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:47.600
 clever, but we also gave them right to fail. So we didn't demand storage. We didn't say, hey,

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:54.160
 hold on to packets all over the place. Just store up piles of packets. Piles of packets

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:58.880
 would pile up in these. We don't ask that. No router has to keep the packets. As a matter of

00:05:58.880 --> 00:06:04.400
 fact, routers are supposed to throw packets away to communicate back and forth between the system

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:10.640
 that maybe things aren't working so well and don't use me to get to California. This, on the other

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:17.120
 hand, with hundreds, maybe, you know, hundreds of thousands or millions of routers, but billions of

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:23.120
 computers on the outside. So we need a way to make this reliable. So we need to have memory to store

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:29.200
 the packets while they're in flight so we can retransmit, but we store the packets in the computers

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:35.360
 that are outside. And there are billions of these computers. There are billions of them. Every

00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:44.400
 computer that you carry around, every laptop. Every time we add another computer to the network,

00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:53.440
 we add storage for packets that are being sent. So when your computer or phone is sending across

00:06:53.440 --> 00:07:00.320
 the network, it is responsible for retaining its own copies. It does not expect the inner part of

00:07:00.320 --> 00:07:08.160
 the network to do so. And that is absolutely brilliant. So it's what really makes this work.

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:15.680
 And so that's kind of a great oversimplification of what's going on. And it turns out that there

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:18.560
 is still a lot of engineering to make that happen.

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:27.360
 So I'd like to introduce you to another of the innovators that we meet in this class. His name

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:33.600
 is Van Jacobson. And I met up with him at Xerox PARC. He did the work we're going to talk about

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:40.880
 while he was at Berkeley. And in the late 1980s, there was this prediction that the internet was

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:47.360
 going to die. And it seems obvious that it's a good idea now. But back then, there was a bunch

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:55.600
 of folks that felt like academics weren't smart enough to make a network. And they were like IBM

00:07:55.600 --> 00:07:59.120
 and Digital Equipment that thought they, the vendors, should make the network and we should

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:07.280
 just pay them to use their network. And as the NSF net was coming up, and more and more computers

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:13.120
 were being connected and the backbone was so slow, it started to fail. And it looked like the

00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:18.160
 predictions of all of the computer vendors that said academics couldn't build a robust, scalable

00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:24.800
 network were going to be true. And so Van Jacobson tells the story very differently. But the way I

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:32.000
 saw the story happening and unfolding in 1987 is Van Jacobson saved us. The network was crashing.

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:38.480
 And we all installed Van Jacobson patches and the network got better. And in my recollection,

00:08:38.480 --> 00:08:43.360
 this was the last time it appeared that the entire internet was going to crash.

00:08:43.360 --> 00:08:49.600
 So we named it the Van Jacobson Protocol. And he doesn't like to call it the Van Jacobson Protocol

00:08:49.600 --> 00:08:59.040
 because he's a shy and unassuming guy. But I think he saved us. And so this interview is

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:06.640
 him describing sort of that moment back in the late '80s where he invented the slow start algorithm

00:09:06.640 --> 00:09:11.920
 that really is part and parcel of every TCP implementation that you have on every computer

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:16.960
 that you use. As a matter of fact, it's being used to do flow control on this lecture right this

00:09:16.960 --> 00:09:21.720
 second as we speak. So here's Van Jacobson.

